      <article class="toc-content partners-body">

        <h2 id="pitch">How we fit together</h2>
        <p>I do one thing well: custom-coded websites for small service businesses. The owners I serve often also need paid ads, SEO, content strategy, branding, copywriting, or coaching, which I leave to others.</p>
        <p>If you offer one of those, I send you owners who need strategy, and you send me owners who need a website. The Partner Program makes it formal, with a named relationship, tracked referrals, and account credit.</p>

        <h2 id="terms">The terms</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>One month's credit, $175,</strong> for every client you refer who signs up for the $175-a-month plan. It's added to your ledger when their first payment clears.</li>
          <li><strong>No cap.</strong> Refer 20 clients in a year, and you earn credit on all 20.</li>
          <li><strong>Tracked by name</strong>, not by a cookie or a link that expires. The client names you on the call form or the onboarding form, I tag you, and you can see your ledger at any time.</li>
          <li><strong>Credit, not cash.</strong> Use it on any work with me: your own monthly plan, hourly work, a future custom build, training, or a referred client's account. Your balance never expires.</li>
          <li><strong>No exclusivity.</strong> You're free to refer to other developers too. I only ask that you think of me when the right client comes along.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="fit">Who it's for</h2>
        <p>The program suits anyone whose clients are small service businesses:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Marketing agencies</strong> that run ads, SEO, or social media for service businesses but don't build sites. If you already recommend a friend for websites, this makes it formal and earns you credit.</li>
          <li><strong>SEO consultants</strong> who review small-business sites and often conclude, "Your site needs a rebuild before any of this SEO work matters."</li>
          <li><strong>Business coaches</strong> whose clients want a good website but don't know whom to trust.</li>
          <li><strong>Accountants and bookkeepers</strong> who often hear from small contractors and inspectors, "I need a better website, but I don't know where to start."</li>
          <li><strong>Industry-association members</strong> (NRCA, NAHI, NACHI, NEC, BIA, and others) who work with service-business owners and trust them to send work back.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>It isn't a fit for lead resellers (Angi, HomeAdvisor, Thumbtack), website flippers, or companies that build sites themselves.</p>

        <h2 id="delivery">What your client gets</h2>
        <p>So you know what you're referring:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>A custom-coded site, written from scratch. <a href="/samples/" class="inline-link">Six live demo sites</a> show the range.</li>
          <li>$175 a month with $0 down, and work begins when the first payment clears.</li>
          <li>Launch in two to three weeks.</li>
          <li>95 to 100 on Google's speed test, on every site.</li>
          <li>Hosting, the security certificate (SSL), security monitoring, and unlimited content updates, all included.</li>
          <li>Direct support: the client emails me, not a help desk.</li>
          <li>Accessibility that meets WCAG 2.2 AA.</li>
          <li>Business details marked up for Google (local-business schema) and on-page SEO that supports your own SEO work.</li>
          <li>A domain registered in the client's name, under the client's control.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="refer">How to refer</h2>
        <p>Three ways, in the order I prefer:</p>
        <ol class="partners-ol">
          <li><strong>A warm introduction by email.</strong> This works best. Copy me on a one-line introduction, and I take it from there.</li>
          <li><strong>Ask the client to mention you.</strong> They book a free call on the <a href="/contact/" class="inline-link">contact page</a> and write your name in the "tell me about your business" field. I credit you on the call.</li>
          <li><strong>Use the referral form.</strong> Add your details and the client's on the <a href="/refer/" class="inline-link">referral form</a>, and I'll contact them and mention you.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2 id="onboarding">How to join</h2>
        <p>If this sounds like a fit:</p>
        <ol class="partners-ol">
          <li>Send the <a href="#apply" class="inline-link">Partner Program application</a> at the bottom of this page.</li>
          <li>We have a 20-minute call to check that your clients and I are a good match.</li>
          <li>You sign the plain-English <a href="/partner-agreement/" class="inline-link">partner agreement</a> online. Its terms are public.</li>
          <li>I add you as a referrer, and every client you send is tagged to you.</li>
          <li>Every quarter, I send a short report on your referrals, signings, and credit earned this year.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2 id="faq">Common questions</h2>
        <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
          <div class="faq-item">
            <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">Why one month's credit? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
            <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">It's simple to track, and it matches what a client earns for a referral: one month of the plan for each person you send who signs up. Twenty referrals in a year add $3,500 of credit.</div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">Why credit instead of cash? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
            <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">It keeps things simple. If you become a client too, your credit pays your own invoices. It avoids the tax and money-handling work of cash payouts, with no 1099-NEC filings, payment holds, or banking changes. And it adds up, so partners who refer steadily build credit for their own work, training, or a referred client's discount.</div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">What if a client cancels in their fourth month? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
            <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">You keep the credit from the first three months. Credit stops after that, because the client is no longer paying, and I never take back credit you've earned or used.</div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">Can I be both a client and a partner? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
            <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Yes. Each role has its own paperwork, and your referral credit pays down your own invoice automatically.</div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">Is there a co-marketing part? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
            <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Once three or more clients you referred have signed, I'll list you on this site as a recommended partner, with your permission. Co-marketing grows out of the relationship over time.</div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">What if a client names a different referrer from the one who sent them? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
            <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">I credit whoever the client names. If you're sure you sent them, tell me through the form below, and I'll check the email trail. Disputes are rare, and the records settle them.</div></div>
          </div>
        </div>

